WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.330 --> 00:00:02.280
PRIYANKA: Welcome to
"Get Cooking in Cloud,"

00:00:02.280 --> 00:00:05.590
where we share the best recipes
to apply in your cloud kitchen.

00:00:05.590 --> 00:00:08.050
I'm Priyanka, and the
next few episodes,

00:00:08.050 --> 00:00:10.650
we will talk about mobile
app backend solutions.

00:00:10.650 --> 00:00:14.010
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:20.250 --> 00:00:23.190
So today I've invited
Jen Person to the kitchen

00:00:23.190 --> 00:00:26.360
to show us how to get cooking
with the mobile app backend.

00:00:26.360 --> 00:00:28.270
Jen, thank you so
much for coming.

00:00:28.270 --> 00:00:29.820
JEN: My pleasure, Priyanka.

00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:32.710
I've brought an appetizing
solution with me today.

00:00:32.710 --> 00:00:33.840
PRIYANKA: Ah, amazing.

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:36.060
And actually, I've
got an app that I

00:00:36.060 --> 00:00:37.890
would like to try this on.

00:00:37.890 --> 00:00:39.870
It's called
"Priyanka's Kitchen,"

00:00:39.870 --> 00:00:42.690
and it's a cooperative
cooking game for mobile.

00:00:42.690 --> 00:00:44.442
JEN: I love that kind of game.

00:00:44.442 --> 00:00:45.150
PRIYANKA: Me too.

00:00:45.150 --> 00:00:47.670
And apparently lots of
other folks like it too.

00:00:47.670 --> 00:00:49.260
"Priyanka's Kitchen"
is experiencing

00:00:49.260 --> 00:00:51.970
a tremendous growth
in App Store and Play

00:00:51.970 --> 00:00:55.650
Store, which is awesome, but
I'm seeing a huge increase

00:00:55.650 --> 00:00:57.990
in the number of files
my app needs to store

00:00:57.990 --> 00:01:00.270
and the amount of data
I need to quickly sync

00:01:00.270 --> 00:01:02.250
between multiple users.

00:01:02.250 --> 00:01:04.200
It seems mobile has
its own challenges.

00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:06.900
Can you help cook up
such a backend service?

00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:08.700
JEN: That's a great
question, Priyanka.

00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:11.760
Most mobile apps and games
need a backend service

00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:14.970
for things that can't be done
solely on device, like sharing

00:01:14.970 --> 00:01:17.310
and processing data
from multiple users,

00:01:17.310 --> 00:01:19.097
or storing large files.

00:01:19.097 --> 00:01:19.930
PRIYANKA: Very true.

00:01:19.930 --> 00:01:22.100
But here on "Get
Cooking in Cloud,"

00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:25.200
we've shown a lot of
backend service solutions.

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:27.180
What's different about
a mobile backend?

00:01:27.180 --> 00:01:28.950
JEN: You're absolutely right.

00:01:28.950 --> 00:01:31.110
Building a backend
service for a mobile app

00:01:31.110 --> 00:01:33.430
is similar to building
a web-based service.

00:01:33.430 --> 00:01:35.460
But there are some
additional requirements.

00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:38.790
First, it's important to
limit on-device data storage.

00:01:38.790 --> 00:01:41.310
Storage on mobile
devices is limited.

00:01:41.310 --> 00:01:43.710
Users need to be able to use
their storage space the way

00:01:43.710 --> 00:01:44.778
that they want to.

00:01:44.778 --> 00:01:46.320
PRIYANKA: For dog
pictures and memes.

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:47.460
[LAUGHING]

00:01:47.460 --> 00:01:49.410
JEN: Next, a mobile
backend service

00:01:49.410 --> 00:01:52.260
needs to synchronize data
across multiple devices.

00:01:52.260 --> 00:01:54.390
When playing a mobile
game with friends,

00:01:54.390 --> 00:01:57.800
your moves need to sync up
across everyone's devices.

00:01:57.800 --> 00:01:59.220
PRIYANKA: Lags
and jitters aren't

00:01:59.220 --> 00:02:02.640
acceptable for mobile games,
especially when I'm winning.

00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:04.050
[LAUGHING]

00:02:04.050 --> 00:02:06.600
JEN: And we know that the
quality of mobile networks

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:07.530
varies greatly.

00:02:07.530 --> 00:02:09.780
So mobile apps need
to assume times

00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:13.260
with limited or no connectivity,
handling that offline case

00:02:13.260 --> 00:02:13.867
gracefully.

00:02:13.867 --> 00:02:15.450
PRIYANKA: I think
I'm getting a handle

00:02:15.450 --> 00:02:17.460
of these mobile
backend differences,

00:02:17.460 --> 00:02:20.250
like how mobile devices
and services need to send

00:02:20.250 --> 00:02:22.050
notifications and messages.

00:02:22.050 --> 00:02:23.213
JEN: Exactly.

00:02:23.213 --> 00:02:24.880
PRIYANKA: And of
course, mobile backends

00:02:24.880 --> 00:02:26.760
should minimize battery drain.

00:02:26.760 --> 00:02:27.850
JEN: You got it.

00:02:27.850 --> 00:02:30.840
So as you can see, there
are some key requirements

00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:32.130
we have to consider.

00:02:32.130 --> 00:02:34.680
And I've cooked up a few
different design patterns

00:02:34.680 --> 00:02:37.320
to demonstrate ways to
use Google Cloud Platform

00:02:37.320 --> 00:02:39.150
to address these requirements.

00:02:39.150 --> 00:02:42.870
For today's recipe, I
brought along some Firebase.

00:02:42.870 --> 00:02:44.280
PRIYANKA: Sounds spicy.

00:02:44.280 --> 00:02:45.600
JEN: Doesn't it?

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:48.360
Even though Firebase sounds
like just one ingredient,

00:02:48.360 --> 00:02:51.120
it definitely isn't
a bland solution.

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:53.340
Firebase is a fully
managed platform

00:02:53.340 --> 00:02:56.220
for building iOS,
Android, and web apps.

00:02:56.220 --> 00:02:59.010
It provides automatic
data synchronization,

00:02:59.010 --> 00:03:02.910
authentication services,
messaging, file storage,

00:03:02.910 --> 00:03:04.782
analytics, and more.

00:03:04.782 --> 00:03:06.240
PRIYANKA: So starting
with Firebase

00:03:06.240 --> 00:03:09.000
seems to be an efficient
way to build or prototype

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:10.870
mobile backend services.

00:03:10.870 --> 00:03:13.890
Now, when is a good
time to serve Firebase?

00:03:13.890 --> 00:03:14.940
Family dinners?

00:03:14.940 --> 00:03:15.960
Weddings?

00:03:15.960 --> 00:03:16.812
Friday parties?

00:03:16.812 --> 00:03:17.520
Birthday parties?

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:19.190
[LAUGHING]

00:03:19.190 --> 00:03:19.690
JEN: Yes.

00:03:19.690 --> 00:03:22.710
Firebase is great for
a last-minute dinner.

00:03:22.710 --> 00:03:26.100
You can develop your app
quickly because Firebase client

00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:29.830
SDKs simplify getting backend
services up and running.

00:03:29.830 --> 00:03:31.980
But I recommend
Firebase for all sorts

00:03:31.980 --> 00:03:33.860
of mobile back indications.

00:03:33.860 --> 00:03:36.090
Firebase products
are designed to scale

00:03:36.090 --> 00:03:39.570
so you can use them seamlessly
from prototype to production

00:03:39.570 --> 00:03:41.700
to top app, like
"Priyanka's Kitchen."

00:03:41.700 --> 00:03:44.190
PRIYANKA: So why is Firebase
a great backend solution,

00:03:44.190 --> 00:03:46.230
especially for mobile apps?

00:03:46.230 --> 00:03:49.290
JEN: Well, Firebase addresses
a lot of the unique needs

00:03:49.290 --> 00:03:50.670
of mobile apps.

00:03:50.670 --> 00:03:53.220
It's great at limiting
on-device space usage when

00:03:53.220 --> 00:03:55.650
you manage key value
data in Cloud Firestore

00:03:55.650 --> 00:03:58.770
and files in cloud
storage for Firebase.

00:03:58.770 --> 00:04:01.980
It makes sending notifications
and data messages a snap

00:04:01.980 --> 00:04:04.080
with Firebase Cloud Messaging.

00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:06.870
It offers automated real-time
data synchronization

00:04:06.870 --> 00:04:09.160
across multiple devices.

00:04:09.160 --> 00:04:11.580
It gracefully handles
the offline case

00:04:11.580 --> 00:04:14.010
and offers
authentication of users

00:04:14.010 --> 00:04:16.068
through a variety of
identity providers.

00:04:16.068 --> 00:04:17.610
PRIYANKA: Firebase
sounds like an out

00:04:17.610 --> 00:04:20.579
of the box solution for many
mobile app backend needs.

00:04:20.579 --> 00:04:22.510
I can't wait to
try it out myself.

00:04:22.510 --> 00:04:25.170
But before making Firebase
my backend solution

00:04:25.170 --> 00:04:27.300
for "Priyanka's Kitchen,"
is there anything

00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:28.600
else I should know?

00:04:28.600 --> 00:04:29.160
JEN: Yes.

00:04:29.160 --> 00:04:31.410
So I don't always
recommend Firebase

00:04:31.410 --> 00:04:33.480
for apps that need
a backend service

00:04:33.480 --> 00:04:35.160
to modify the synchronized data.

00:04:35.160 --> 00:04:35.820
PRIYANKA: Hm.

00:04:35.820 --> 00:04:36.690
That's interesting.

00:04:36.690 --> 00:04:37.980
Give me an example.

00:04:37.980 --> 00:04:40.020
JEN: Firebase
products handle a lot

00:04:40.020 --> 00:04:42.000
of the heavy backend
lifting for you.

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:44.140
So you don't have to build
some features yourself.

00:04:44.140 --> 00:04:45.690
But there are still
situations where

00:04:45.690 --> 00:04:48.760
you may need to modify
data outside of the client.

00:04:48.760 --> 00:04:52.140
For example, if "Priyanka's
Kitchen" keeps a leaderboard,

00:04:52.140 --> 00:04:55.163
you'll want to run the code
to rank users in a server.

00:04:55.163 --> 00:04:56.080
PRIYANKA: That's true.

00:04:56.080 --> 00:04:58.200
I do need to keep the
leaderboard up to date.

00:04:58.200 --> 00:05:02.350
But Firebase does have server
SDKs called the admin SDKs.

00:05:02.350 --> 00:05:04.500
Does Firebase have
a server solution

00:05:04.500 --> 00:05:06.690
in which to use these SDKs?

00:05:06.690 --> 00:05:08.290
JEN: That's a great question.

00:05:08.290 --> 00:05:09.990
Well, one of the many
Firebase products

00:05:09.990 --> 00:05:13.890
is a serverless solution called
Cloud Functions for Firebase.

00:05:13.890 --> 00:05:15.930
Cloud Functions let
you automatically

00:05:15.930 --> 00:05:18.060
run backend code in
response to events

00:05:18.060 --> 00:05:22.220
triggered by Firebase
features and HTTPS requests.

00:05:22.220 --> 00:05:25.160
You can use the admin
SDKs in a Cloud Function

00:05:25.160 --> 00:05:28.640
to modify the synchronized
data, among other tasks.

00:05:28.640 --> 00:05:32.270
PRIYANKA: So I could use Cloud
Functions to update user ranks?

00:05:32.270 --> 00:05:34.140
JEN: Yes, you certainly can.

00:05:34.140 --> 00:05:36.350
But as your app
grows, this can get

00:05:36.350 --> 00:05:38.420
more challenging to run
from a Cloud Function.

00:05:38.420 --> 00:05:39.960
PRIYANKA: Ah, so let me guess.

00:05:39.960 --> 00:05:43.527
Is it because Cloud
Functions are short-lived?

00:05:43.527 --> 00:05:44.360
JEN: You guessed it.

00:05:44.360 --> 00:05:46.560
Cloud Functions are short-lived.

00:05:46.560 --> 00:05:49.190
So if you have a small amount
of user scores to process,

00:05:49.190 --> 00:05:52.190
you can rank them quickly
before the function tears down.

00:05:52.190 --> 00:05:54.230
But when you have
lots of user scores

00:05:54.230 --> 00:05:56.870
to analyze, say,
hundreds of thousands,

00:05:56.870 --> 00:05:59.720
then a long-running
server is a better fit.

00:05:59.720 --> 00:06:02.030
In that case, Firebase
alone will not

00:06:02.030 --> 00:06:03.560
be a complete solution.

00:06:03.560 --> 00:06:07.040
I'll talk about that
recipe another day.

00:06:07.040 --> 00:06:08.960
PRIYANKA: Aw, you're killing me.

00:06:08.960 --> 00:06:10.190
Wow, you are right.

00:06:10.190 --> 00:06:12.930
Firebase isn't a
bland solution at all.

00:06:12.930 --> 00:06:14.980
It seems like
there's a lot to it,

00:06:14.980 --> 00:06:17.300
so it can be hard to picture.

00:06:17.300 --> 00:06:19.490
I would love to see
what a complete Firebase

00:06:19.490 --> 00:06:21.560
solution looks like.

00:06:21.560 --> 00:06:24.170
JEN: Well, I just happen to have
a completed Firebase solution

00:06:24.170 --> 00:06:25.760
right here.

00:06:25.760 --> 00:06:28.740
Here you can see a diagram
of our design pattern.

00:06:28.740 --> 00:06:31.370
And right here is Friendly Pix.

00:06:31.370 --> 00:06:33.170
It's a sample app
that features examples

00:06:33.170 --> 00:06:35.780
of how to use many
different Firebase products.

00:06:35.780 --> 00:06:37.020
PRIYANKA: Looks amazing.

00:06:37.020 --> 00:06:38.690
So what should our
viewers at home

00:06:38.690 --> 00:06:42.740
do if they would like to cook
up their own Firebase app?

00:06:42.740 --> 00:06:45.500
JEN: You can check out the
guides, reference docs,

00:06:45.500 --> 00:06:48.602
code examples on GitHub, and
the Firebase YouTube channel.

00:06:48.602 --> 00:06:50.060
PRIYANKA: There
you have it, folks.

00:06:50.060 --> 00:06:52.280
That's all for today on
"Get Cooking in Cloud."

00:06:52.280 --> 00:06:55.050
Here's hoping you can
whip up something great.

00:06:55.050 --> 00:06:58.220
Join us next time because Jen
will be coming back to show us

00:06:58.220 --> 00:07:00.680
another tasty mobile
app backend solution.

00:07:00.680 --> 00:07:02.120
If you liked this
video and would

00:07:02.120 --> 00:07:03.757
like to see more
of such content,

00:07:03.757 --> 00:07:05.840
don't forget to like and
subscribe to our channel!

00:07:05.840 --> 00:07:09.190
[MUSIC PLAYING]

